---
description: General Next.js and Supabase development rules
alwaysApply: true
---

# Required Stack

Use:

- Next.js App Router
- React
- TypeScript strict mode
- Tailwind CSS
- Supabase PostgreSQL
- Supabase Auth
- Supabase Storage
- Supabase RLS
- Resend
- Vercel
- Zod
- React Hook Form for complex forms

Do not introduce alternative frameworks, databases, authentication providers or UI libraries without approval.

# Code Rules

- Do not use `any`.
- Use explicit TypeScript types.
- Use `unknown` when input type is not known.
- Do not use `@ts-ignore`.
- Keep components focused and reasonably small.
- Avoid duplicated business logic.
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions.
- Use descriptive English names for files, variables and database fields.
- Remove unused code, console logs and dead files.
- Do not use inline styles.
- Do not hard-code repeated values when a constant is appropriate.

# Next.js Rules

- Prefer Server Components.
- Use Client Components only when browser interaction is required.
- Use Server Actions or Route Handlers for mutations.
- Protect admin operations on the server.
- Do not rely only on hidden buttons or client-side route protection.
- Use loading, error and empty states where relevant.

# Forms

Every form must include:

- Client-side validation for user experience.
- Server-side validation with Zod.
- Hebrew validation messages.
- Loading state.
- Disabled submit button while processing.
- Error feedback.
- Success feedback.
- Prevention of duplicate submission where relevant.

# Supabase

- Use the official Supabase client.
- Use separate browser and server clients.
- Use `@supabase/ssr` with cookies.
- Enable RLS on every application table.
- Add explicit Storage policies.
- Never expose the Service Role key to the browser.
- Select only required columns.
- Use pagination for growing lists.

# Interface

- All interfaces are in Hebrew.
- The entire application is RTL.
- Set `lang="he"` and `dir="rtl"` in the root layout.
- User-facing messages are in Hebrew.
- Technical code and names are in English.
- All pages must be responsive and accessible.